{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context\n\nMake the bridge execution path portable across local shells, GitHub Actions, Jenkins, GitLab CI,\nand bare environments without breaking checked-out workflows or environments that still use the\nlegacy reconcile vocabulary. The current production workflow contains about 185 lines of runner\nlogic, and `src/rebar/_commands/fsck_repair.py` shells out to `gh workflow disable/enable` without a\nrepository argument. That best-effort GitHub-only schedule control silently disappears outside GHA.\n\nThe bridge/reconciler execution path is otherwise CI-neutral. The repository as a whole is not:\n`src/rebar/mirror_guard.py` intentionally reads `GITHUB_TOKEN`, hard-codes the GitHub mirror, and\ncalls `api.github.com`; `src/rebar/llm/usage_log.py` and `usage_report.py` optionally write\n`GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY`. Those are GitHub-specific mirror-health/reporting features, not bridge\nexecution, and are documented out of scope rather than used as evidence of bridge portability.\n\nThe parent epic makes GitLab a required portability proof even though this repository is not\ncurrently hosted there. Ship a thin committed `.gitlab-ci.yml` as a contract artifact over the same\nrunner-neutral entrypoint. Validate it offline against a pinned vendored copy of GitLab's official\nCI JSON schema; record the schema's upstream URL, upstream revision, retrieval date, and checksum in\nan adjacent provenance file. The GitLab artifact is exercised structurally and through its extracted\nshell body rather than by standing up a shared runner.\n\n## Runner-neutral entrypoint\n\nCreate `scripts/run_reconcile_bridge.py` as the one executable bridge wrapper. It selects only the\ncanonical `rebar bridge preview` or `rebar bridge sync` operation, owns the canonical wrapper\ncontract (`CANONICAL_EXIT_CODES`, required environment names, and a 60-minute/3600-second timeout),\nrejects shallow clones, and contains no GitHub/Jenkins/GitLab branch.\n`.github/workflows/reconcile-bridge.yml`, `Jenkinsfile`, `.gitlab-ci.yml`, and local documentation\ninvoke that exact file. Provider wrappers retain only checkout, credentials, timeout,\nartifact/masking syntax, and the platform-specific expression that maps the entrypoint exit status\nto job success/failure. Each provider timeout is derived from or contract-checked against the\nrunner's 3600-second constant rather than copied as an unchecked value.\n\nThe entrypoint calls the checkout build and canonical bridge route. Legacy `rebar reconcile --mode`\nand direct-engine routes remain untouched, including their 3/4/75 results, so older workflow\nrevisions and environments continue to run during migration.\n\n`scripts/run_reconcile_bridge.py` is the wrapper source of truth for the secret-name set and timeout.\nIts allowed exits are derived and cross-checked against\n`src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/__main__.py::_Disposition`, whose canonical column is 0/1/2.\nContract tests import the runner constants, parse all three provider wrappers, and compare their\nexit-code table, secret-name set, and timeout; the tests do not restate a second table.\n\n## Portable destructive-repair control\n\nReplace the `gh workflow disable/enable` helpers with the durable control already shipped by story\n`1104`: `refs/reconciler/gate` pause documents written through\n`rebar_reconciler._ref_lock.set_pause` and cleared through `clear_gate`, using the same configured\nremote and identity behavior as `rebar bridge pause/resume`. A scheduled pass on GitHub, Jenkins,\nor GitLab reads that ref before mutation and exits benignly while paused.\n\nThe repair sequence is explicit and fail-closed:\n\n1. Read the durable gate. If another actor already paused the bridge, abort the repair and do not\n   clear that pause. Otherwise generate a repair-unique reason/token, CAS-write it, and set a local\n   `pause_created` flag only after the CAS succeeds. Any transport, authentication, corruption, or\n   CAS uncertainty aborts the destructive repair.\n2. Run the existing provider-neutral `_reconciler_in_flight` check against\n   `refs/reconciler/lock`. HELD, corrupt, timeout, or indeterminate aborts the repair.\n3. Run the repair.\n4. In `finally`, clear only when `pause_created` is true and the current ref still contains this\n   repair's unique reason/token. Use the observed ref as the CAS predecessor. A foreign or changed\n   pause is never cleared; a clear failure is surfaced and leaves the bridge paused for operator\n   recovery rather than silently reopening it.\n\nThe lock remains the in-flight safety check; the durable gate is the schedule pause. This preserves\nthe currently-working GitHub safety behavior without a provider API and closes the Jenkins/GitLab\nrace.\n\n## Verification strategy\n\n- Run the real entrypoint in temporary repositories with vendor variables scrubbed and with\n  representative local, GitHub Actions, Jenkins, GitLab, and bare environments. Results and\n  repository/ref postconditions must be identical, and `gh` is absent from `PATH`.\n- Use a bare git remote to set the durable pause, attempt a canonical sync, and prove the pass exits\n  benignly without mutation. Exercise pause write failure, pre-existing foreign pause,\n  corrupt/indeterminate lock, ownership change before clear, and resume failure and prove destructive\n  repair fails closed without clearing another actor's pause.\n- Create a real shallow clone (or drive `git rev-parse --is-shallow-repository` to `true`) and assert\n  the entrypoint exits non-zero with the named `f193` error before any bridge operation runs.\n- Parse the production GHA, Jenkins, and GitLab wrappers and execute their extracted shell bodies.\n  The Jenkins `sh` body is a single-quoted Groovy string so secrets reach the shell/masking layer\n  without GString interpolation. Validate `.gitlab-ci.yml` offline with the existing `jsonschema`\n  dependency against the vendored official schema and verify its provenance metadata/checksum.\n- `shellcheck` is a pinned CI/dev prerequisite installed by the verification workflow. The contract\n  test hard-fails with a setup error when the binary is missing; it never skips. Shellcheck runs over\n  the script bodies extracted from all three configs, and a malformed wrapper/config fixture must\n  make extraction, schema validation, or lint fail.\n- The production GHA workflow is still the scheduled artifact rebar runs, so the shared entrypoint is\n  exercised by the existing 20-minute schedule. No Jenkins or GitLab controller is stood up: both\n  committed configs use the same extracted-body, offline-schema, and contract checks.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `scripts/run_reconcile_bridge.py` is the one runner-neutral bridge entrypoint; GHA, Jenkins,\n      and GitLab wrappers contain only checkout, credentials, timeout, masking/artifacts, and exit\n      mapping.\n- [ ] Checked-out legacy workflows and old-vocabulary environments retain the legacy reconcile\n      commands, defaults, messages, and 3/4/75 behavior.\n- [ ] `fsck --repair` uses the durable pause ref plus the fail-closed lock check and requires no `gh`\n      binary; pause/lock/resume uncertainty never permits a destructive repair to proceed silently.\n- [ ] A real bare-remote test proves the durable pause blocks canonical sync identically from local,\n      GHA-shaped, Jenkins-shaped, and GitLab-shaped environments; an existing foreign pause is never\n      cleared, and only a repair-owned unique pause may be cleared by CAS.\n- [ ] A five-environment contract matrix runs the real entrypoint under scrubbed\n      local/GHA/Jenkins/GitLab/bare environments, with `gh` absent, and asserts identical\n      exit/output/ref effects and no vendor-variable reads.\n- [ ] A shallow-clone test drives the true shallow condition and asserts the named `f193` error and\n      non-zero exit before reconciliation.\n- [ ] The shipped GHA artifact invokes the shared entrypoint and remains the workflow exercised on\n      the existing schedule; committed `Jenkinsfile` and `.gitlab-ci.yml` artifacts invoke the same\n      entrypoint.\n- [ ] All three provider configs are cross-checked against the runner constants and `_Disposition`\n      for one exit-code table, one secret-name set, and the 60-minute timeout, never against\n      duplicated unchecked tables.\n- [ ] `.gitlab-ci.yml` validates offline against a pinned vendored official GitLab JSON schema whose\n      source URL, upstream revision, retrieval date, and checksum are committed.\n- [ ] Jenkins shell bodies are single-quoted; pinned `shellcheck` runs over extracted script bodies\n      from all three configs in CI, fails when unavailable, and rejects malformed extracted\n      script/config fixtures.\n- [ ] GitHub-only mirror/report helpers are documented as out of bridge-execution scope.\n\n## File impact\n\n- `scripts/run_reconcile_bridge.py`: runner-neutral entrypoint and wrapper contract.\n- `.github/workflows/reconcile-bridge.yml`: thin production GHA wrapper.\n- `.github/workflows/_build-and-test.yml`: install pinned shellcheck for contract verification.\n- `Jenkinsfile`: thin committed Jenkins wrapper with safe credentials.\n- `.gitlab-ci.yml`: thin committed GitLab wrapper over the shared entrypoint.\n- `.github/schemas/gitlab-ci.schema.json` and `.github/schemas/gitlab-ci.schema.provenance.json`:\n  pinned official schema plus source URL, revision, retrieval date, and checksum for offline\n  validation.\n- `src/rebar/_commands/fsck_repair.py`: durable repair-owned pause plus fail-closed lock safety\n  without `gh`.\n- focused workflow/runner/fsck tests: five-environment parity, three-config schema/contract/shell\n  validation, shallow rejection, and pause ownership/lock/resume fail-closed behavior.\n- `docs/user-guide.md` and `docs/release-notes.md`: local/GHA/Jenkins/GitLab usage, legacy continuity,\n  and GitHub-specific known gaps."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786291937155259001,"uuid":"835f3205-92ea-4c47-b34f-c343dff347d1"}